INT. ROOFTOP CONSERVATORY - EVENING

Glass and ironwork. Orchids in terra-cotta pots. A string quartet tunes in the corner.

SOLANGE MARCHETTI (30s, emerald dress) accepts a champagne flute while her fiancé, ARTHUR BEAUMONT (30s, tuxedo) is intercepted by his mother, CORDELIA BEAUMONT (60s, pearls), who plucks a stray thread from his lapel as the cellist, LEOPOLD VASS (20s, nervous), drops his bow and scrambles after it, and the violist, HANNELORE KRUG (40s, steel-rimmed glasses), steadies a music stand that Leopold's elbow almost topples, and Solange, observing all this, steps backward into a potted fern.

SOLANGE
Sorry, ferns. Sorry.

ARTHUR
You all right?

SOLANGE
Splendid.

At the door, LAZARUS RENNICK (30s, rumpled suit, a mysterious envelope) appears. He catches her eye, taps his chest twice. She stiffens. Arthur doesn't notice.

CORDELIA
Arthur, stand straight.

ARTHUR
I am standing straight, Mother.

CORDELIA
Straighter.

SOLANGE
Arthur, I need a moment.

ARTHUR ^
Solange, I need a moment.

Both laugh, awkwardly. Cordelia lifts an eyebrow. Hannelore sets the stand down and glances toward the door. Lazarus is gone. The envelope sits on the threshold, white against the dark tiles.

SOLANGE
I'll be right back.

She crosses the conservatory quickly, snatches the envelope, tears it open. Her face drains of color. Behind her, the quartet strikes up the first bars of a waltz, bright and cheerful.
